{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-0fb72c91-1b1a-4a91-90a4-3b78d7f6cad5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-a1d2a4761959e62b","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8010-70gyc9k5/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8010-E003.md","content":"# Document and publish the structured reconciliation log field convention\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic delivers the structured reconciliation log field convention as a documented contribution guideline and publishes it for external operator adoption. The guideline specifies the required structured fields (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`), the camelCase naming rationale (alignment with controller-runtime's structured log key conventions), and the three-problem diagnosis (message-embedded resource identity, non-standard DSCI key names, missing `resourceKind`), with before/after code examples drawn from the completed conversions and a DSCI key-migration guide for operators holding queries against the old field names. It is published as a specification that external operator teams (Cluster Observability Operator: Loki Operator, Tempo Operator) may adopt voluntarily per platform policy overlay 0008.\n\n## Scope\n\nContent outline (authored inside the contribution guideline):\n\n- Required structured fields for reconciliation error logs: `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`, including handling of cluster-scoped resources (namespace omitted or empty).\n- camelCase field-naming rationale and alignment with controller-runtime/logr conventions.\n- The three-problem diagnosis: message-embedded identity, non-standard DSCI keys (`Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization`), missing `resourceKind`.\n- Before/after code examples for converting message-embedded identity to structured key-value pairs, reflecting the implemented conversions in the manager and cloudmanager binaries.\n- DSCI key-name migration guide: release-notes-ready guidance with before/after Loki/CloudWatch query examples, noting the single-release transition.\n- Guidance for new controller development (what a new controller's error logs must emit).\n- Verification workflow for the CI lint rule that guards the convention.\n\nPublication activities:\n\n- Publish the convention as a specification for voluntary adoption by external operator teams (Cluster Observability Operator: Loki Operator, Tempo Operator), and file tracking issues in the relevant COO projects.\n- Record the open product question on optional extension fields beyond name/namespace/kind (e.g., reconciliation attempt count, error category — owner: PM / Observability team) as a explicitly deferred decision: the guideline's required-field set is fixed by this strategy; extension fields, if adopted, are follow-up scope.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- The contribution guideline is published, specifying required fields (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`), the camelCase naming rationale, and the three-problem diagnosis.\n- The guideline includes before/after code examples for converting message-embedded identity to structured key-value pairs, and these examples match the implemented conversion patterns in the rhods-operator manager and cloudmanager binaries.\n- The guideline includes a DSCI key-migration section with before/after query examples for log consumers.\n- The guideline is technically reviewed against the implemented conversions (reviewer confirms examples and field names match the shipped code) before publication.\n- The convention is published for external operator adoption and tracking issues are filed in the Cluster Observability Operator projects (Loki Operator, Tempo Operator); adoption itself remains at those teams' discretion and is not a completion condition.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-8 (P1) — document the convention as a contribution guideline for new controller development and publish for external operator adoption.\n"}}],"model":"r