{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-863a76d0074a02cb","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8010\n3\tepic_count: 3\n4\tcritical_path_length: 2\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary: RHAISTRAT-8010\n11\t\n12\tOperator reconciliation log standardization with structured resource context — convert message-embedded resource identity to structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`) across the rhods-operator's manager and cloudmanager binaries, migrate the DSCI controller off non-standard key names, and document/publish the convention.\n13\t\n14\t## Triage Decision\n15\t\n16\tNot below-threshold: although the strategy is S-sized, single-component (rhods-operator), and single-team (ODH Operator team), the scope spans two architecturally distinct work streams — mechanical code conversion across two binaries and substantive documentation authoring (a new contribution guideline defining the convention, plus publication for external operator adoption, HLR-8 / effort item 4). Per the multiple-distinct-work-streams escape condition, full decomposition proceeds. Not docs-only: rhods-operator requires code changes.\n17\t\n18\t## Staff Engineer Input Overrides\n19\t\n20\tThe Staff Engineer / SME Input section is non-empty and takes precedence; it corrects the AI-generated technical approach. The strategy body already reflects the corrected facts; the decomposition follows them and logs the overrides here for traceability:\n21\t\n22\t- **Action file count corrected**: \"~16 component action files\" is wrong — there are exactly 15 action files across four tiers (8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3 module). All 8 components have exactly one action file.\n23\t- **Module controller tier added**: the module controller with its 8 module handlers (Dashboard, AIGateway, KServe, OGX, Workbenches, MLflowOperator, FeastOperator, MCPLifecycleOperator) and its 3 action files is in scope (HLR on module controller error logs is P0).\n24\t- **Open question on action-file counts removed**: the premise (\"not all 8 components have action files\") was wrong; the count is confirmed 8/8.\n25\t- **Confirmed as authoritative**: the three-problem diagnosis (message-embedded identity, DSCI non-standard keys `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization`, missing `resourceKind`), camelCase field naming, cloudmanager's shared reconciler already providing `name`/`namespace`, and the cluster-scoped-resource acceptance criterion.\n26\t\n27\t## Epic List\n28\t\n29\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n30\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n31\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | Convert manager binary reconciliation error logs to structured resource context | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n32\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E002 | Convert cloudmanager binary reconciliation error logs to structured resource context | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n33\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E003 | Document and publish the structured reconciliation log field convention | Implementation (docs-authoring) | ODH Operator team | P1 |\n34\t\n35\t## Dependency DAG\n36\t\n37\t```mermaid\n38\tgraph TD\n39\t    E001[RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 manager binary conversion]\n40\t    E002[RHAISTRAT-8010-E002 cloudmanager binary conversion]\n41\t    E003[RHAISTRAT-8010-E003 convention documentation]\n42\t    E001 --> E003\n43\t    E002 --> E003\n44\t```\n45\t\n46\t## DAG Justification\n47\t\n48\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n49\t|------|------|-----------|\n50\t| E001 ∥ E002 (no edge) | Rule 15 (same repo, different areas) | Manager and cloudmanager are separate binaries with disjoint controller/file sets; both perform the same mechanical conversion on their own call sites. Merge-conflict risk is coordination, not dependency. |\n51\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring blocked by ALL Implementation epics) | The contribution guideline documents the convention as implemented, including before/after code examples and the DSCI key migration guide, so it must reflect the completed conversions. |\n52\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring blocked by ALL Implementation epics) | The published convention also covers the cloudmanager controllers' shared-reconciler logging behavior; docs describe what was built. |\n53\t\n54\tNotes on edges deliberately NOT drawn:\n55\t\n56\t- **No standalone CI lint-rule epic.** The strategy's CI lint/static-analysis check (acceptance criterion \"measured by CI lint rule or static analysis\" and the risk mitigation \"add a CI lint rule\") is a shared-surface enforcement deliverable. It is divided per surface: each conversion epic authors and verifies the part of the lint rule covering its own binary's converted call sites, as scope plus a verification acceptance criterion.\n57\t- **No edge from the external COO dependency.** COO team adoption (Loki Operator, Tempo Operator) is external, voluntary, and explicitly outside RHOAI's control per platform policy overlay 0008; no epic in this strategy is gated on it. Publishing the convention (including filing tracking issues in COO projects) is scope on the documentation epic.\n58\t- **No Investigation epics.** All four open questions were evaluated against the decision rule (\"does the answer change which downstream epics exist or what they do?\") — none does:\n59\t  - *Extra fields beyond name/namespace/kind (PM / Observability team)*: the HLRs and acceptance criteria fix the required field set (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`); adopting optional extension fields would be follow-up scope beyond this strategy's requirements, not a change to any epic's approach. Recorded as an open question on the documentation epic.\n60\t  - *Which log-based alerts rely on message-string patterns (SRE / Support)*: the strategy itself schedules this as the log-consumer regression validation step before merge — an acceptance criterion on each conversion epic, with fix-and-retry handling.\n61\t  - *Cloudmanager release phasing (PM / ODH Operator team)*: a scheduling decision; the cloudmanager epic exists and is P0 per HLR-5 either way. Noted in Health Warnings.\n62\t  - *Actual percentage of embedded-identity calls (ODH Operator team)*: the audit is the first scope item of each conversion epic; the answer changes work volume, not epic existence or approach.\n63\t- **No onboarding chain.** rhods-operator is in the platform (architecture context file exists: `rhods-operator.md`); no repo-onboarding, konflux-onboarding, or license-validation epics are generated.\n64\t\n65\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n66\t\n67\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n68\t|-----|----------|---------|\n69\t| HLR-1 — convert message-embedded name/namespace to structured KV pairs in all rhods-operator reconciliation error logs | P0 | E001, E002 |\n70\t| HLR-2 — migrate DSCI controller from `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` to `name`/`namespace` | P0 | E001 |\n71\t| HLR-3 — service controller (Gateway, Auth, Monitoring, CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup) error logs include `resourceKind` | P0 | E001 |\n72\t| HLR-4 — all 15 action-file error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` (8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3 module) | P0 | E001 |\n73\t| HLR-5 — cloudmanager controller (AWS/Azure/CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` | P0 | E002 |\n74\t| HLR-6 — module controller error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` (8 module handlers) | P0 | E001 |\n75\t| HLR-7 — DSC and component controller error logs include `resourceKind` | P1 | E001 (bundled) — same `log.Error` call sites as the HLR-1/HLR-4 conversions in the same files; adding `resourceKind` is one more key-value pair on lines already being edited, so a separate epic would re-audit and re-edit the same files twice; not independently schedulable. |\n76\t| HLR-8 — document the convention as a contribution guideline and publish for external operator adoption | P1 | E003 |\n77\t\n78\tAll 8 HLRs covered; no orphaned requirements.\n79\t\n80\t## Health Warnings\n81\t\n82\t- **E001 size over the ~2-week guideline (managed)**: (1) Effort estimate ~1.25–1.5 sprints by the strategy's effort arithmetic (half of the ~0.5-sprint call-site audit plus the ~0.75–1-sprint manager conversion, and the manager share of the lint rule and unit-test harness). (2) Kept whole because the three problems (embedded-identity conversion, DSCI key migration, `resourceKind` addition) are one mechanical pass over the same `log.Error` call sites in the same files — splitting by controller tier would audit the same files twice and duplicate the lint-rule and test-harness setup. (3) Re-split trigger: the call-site audit exceeds the strategy's 75% embedded-identity estimate or manager conversion effort passes ~1.5 sprints, moving the epic past 2 sprints — then split by controller tier (DSC + 8 component controllers vs. module/DSCI/service tiers), each keeping its share of the audit, lint coverage, and unit tests.\n83\t- **Cloudmanager release phasing unresolved (non-blocking)**: the open question of whether cloudmanager ships in the same release as manager or a subsequent one is a PM scheduling decision; E002 is scoped and P0 per HLR-5 regardless. Deferral changes the release plan, not this decomposition.\n84\t- **Scope trap — alert rules**: out-of-scope excludes custom dashboards and alerting rules, while the backwards-compatibility NFR requires identifying text-based alert rules that rely on message strings. Resolution: the *identification and coordination with SRE/Support* is an acceptance criterion on each conversion epic; *updating* the alert rules themselves is SRE-owned and out of scope.\n85\t- **DSCI key-name migration breaks existing queries**: operators with Loki/CloudWatch queries filtering on `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` will break. Mitigation is folded into the documentation epic (release-notes migration guidance with before/after query examples, single-release transition) — a coordination risk, not a structural one.\n86\t- **Strategy-level process gates pending**: Architecture Review, Documentation Support, and other prerequisites are marked \"Pending human review\" at the strategy level; they gate the strategy's approval, not individual epic structure.\n87\t- **Passive external team**: the Cluster Observability Operator team (Loki Operator, Tempo Operator) only receives the published convention — adoption at their discretion per platform policy overlay 0008. No validation epic is generated because that team performs no validation work for this strategy.\n88\t\n89\t## Tiered Delivery\n90\t\n91\tNot applicable. The only external dependency with uncertain timing (COO team adoption) gates none of this strategy's deliverables — RHOAI's obligation ends at publishing the convention, so there is no dependency-gated Tier 2 work to split out. Both conversion epics deliver independent value in parallel.\n92\t\n93\t## Component Mappings\n94\t\n95\t- The strategy's affected component **rhods-operator** has no verbatim entry in `.context/rhai-components.txt`. Closest canonical name: **OpenShift AI Productization** (the rhods-operator is the RHOAI platform operator that productizes the ODH upstream into OpenShift AI; assigned to E001 and E002). Logged as a non-obvious mapping.\n96\t- The documentation epic (E003) uses the canonical component **Documentation** (Red Hat docs content process work).\n97\t- Both conversion epics span all of the strategy's rhods-operator sub-surfaces (5 manager controller tiers, 15 action files, 3 cloudmanager controllers); the single most-relevant canonical name is assigned per above and the span is noted here.\n98\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8484e03877856064","agent_id":"a9cb5feebeed53395","session_id":"24eb6c29-6a9a-48c1-874c-32ca163b119c","uuid":"e1b18953-926c-4421-9517-e46bb5b6b4ed","timestamp":"2026-10-26T19:43:32.530Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-863a76d0074a02cb","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8010 decomposition"}
