{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bff0d2ae1ce2994b","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8010-E003\n3\ttitle: Document and publish the structured reconciliation log field convention\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8010\n5\tcomponent: Documentation\n6\tteam: ODH Operator team\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P1\n9\timplementation_type: docs-authoring\n10\tdependencies:\n11\t- RHAISTRAT-8010-E001\n12\t- RHAISTRAT-8010-E002\n13\tai_signals:\n14\t  change_specificity: 1\n15\t  pattern_precedent: 0\n16\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n17\t  existing_foundation: 1\n18\t  open_questions: 0\n19\t  external_dependency: 0\n20\t  human_process_gates: -1\n21\t  repo_access: 1\n22\t  architecture_claims: 1\n23\tinvestigation_signals: null\n24\tai_implementability: High\n25\tai_implementability_score: 3\n26\tjira_key: null\n27\tbranch: null\n28\tgated_by: null\n29\tgate_failure_impact: null\n30\t---\n31\t# Document and publish the structured reconciliation log field convention\n32\t\n33\t## Description\n34\t\n35\tThis epic delivers the structured reconciliation log field convention as a documented contribution guideline and publishes it for external operator adoption. The guideline specifies the required structured fields (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`), the camelCase naming rationale (alignment with controller-runtime's structured log key conventions), and the three-problem diagnosis (message-embedded resource identity, non-standard DSCI key names, missing `resourceKind`), with before/after code examples drawn from the completed conversions and a DSCI key-migration guide for operators holding queries against the old field names. It is published as a specification that external operator teams (Cluster Observability Operator: Loki Operator, Tempo Operator) may adopt voluntarily per platform policy overlay 0008.\n36\t\n37\t## Scope\n38\t\n39\tContent outline (authored inside the contribution guideline):\n40\t\n41\t- Required structured fields for reconciliation error logs: `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`, including handling of cluster-scoped resources (namespace omitted or empty).\n42\t- camelCase field-naming rationale and alignment with controller-runtime/logr conventions.\n43\t- The three-problem diagnosis: message-embedded identity, non-standard DSCI keys (`Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization`), missing `resourceKind`.\n44\t- Before/after code examples for converting message-embedded identity to structured key-value pairs, reflecting the implemented conversions in the manager and cloudmanager binaries.\n45\t- DSCI key-name migration guide: release-notes-ready guidance with before/after Loki/CloudWatch query examples, noting the single-release transition.\n46\t- Guidance for new controller development (what a new controller's error logs must emit).\n47\t- Verification workflow for the CI lint rule that guards the convention.\n48\t\n49\tPublication activities:\n50\t\n51\t- Publish the convention as a specification for voluntary adoption by external operator teams (Cluster Observability Operator: Loki Operator, Tempo Operator), and file tracking issues in the relevant COO projects.\n52\t- Record the open product question on optional extension fields beyond name/namespace/kind (e.g., reconciliation attempt count, error category — owner: PM / Observability team) as an explicitly deferred decision: the guideline's required-field set is fixed by this strategy; extension fields, if adopted, are follow-up scope.\n53\t\n54\t## Acceptance Criteria\n55\t\n56\t- The contribution guideline is published, specifying required fields (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`), the camelCase naming rationale, and the three-problem diagnosis.\n57\t- The guideline includes before/after code examples for converting message-embedded identity to structured key-value pairs, and these examples match the implemented conversion patterns in the rhods-operator manager and cloudmanager binaries.\n58\t- The guideline includes a DSCI key-migration section with before/after query examples for log consumers.\n59\t- The guideline is technically reviewed against the implemented conversions (reviewer confirms examples and field names match the shipped code) before publication.\n60\t- The convention is published for external operator adoption and tracking issues are filed in the Cluster Observability Operator projects (Loki Operator, Tempo Operator); adoption itself remains at those teams' discretion and is not a completion condition.\n61\t\n62\t## HLR Traceability\n63\t\n64\t- HLR-8 (P1) — document the convention as a contribution guideline for new controller development and publish for external operator adoption.\n65\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8484e03877856064","agent_id":"a9cb5feebeed53395","session_id":"24eb6c29-6a9a-48c1-874c-32ca163b119c","uuid":"71c22458-c77f-4d19-9793-418f3a466bef","timestamp":"2026-10-26T19:43:37.442Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-bff0d2ae1ce2994b","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8010 decomposition"}
