acts (converted code + lint rule + tests; published guideline). No `gated_by`/`gate_failure_impact` fields are set anywhere, so no gating-metadata consistency issues arise. E003's `implementation_type: docs-authoring` is correct.\n\n### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\nAll three epics are Implementation type and carry `ai_signals` (the 9-signal rubric) — the type-appropriate field; `investigation_signals` is correctly null. Signal values are consistent with the rationales in the ai-signals files. E001's `open_questions: 1` is defensible: the rationale addresses each remaining strategy open question and correctly classifies them as volume/validation concerns rather than approach changes. E003's `open_questions: 0` and `external_dependency: 0` both carry justified rationales (the PM extension-fields question; COO tracking-issue filing). Calibration check on `architecture_claims`: the strategy makes architecture claims (controller tiers, CRDs, cloudmanager binary) and an architecture-context file covers rhods-operator; both E001 and E002 score `architecture_claims: 1` with rationales citing the actual provisioned file (`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/rhods-operator.md`, which exists and covers the cloudmanager binary and controller-runtime/logr usage) — properly calibrated, not a \"no claims\" miss. Computed `ai_implementability`/`ai_implementability_score` values were not checked per review scope.\n\n### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\nEvery epic has testable ACs with explicit measurement methods (lint rule/static analysis, unit tests parsing test logger output, codebase grep). Rule-mandated ACs: E003 (docs-authoring) has the technical-review-before-publication AC; no konflux chain exists; the conversion work is an additive log-call change, not a component replacement, so the rollback/feature-flag rule does not apply (backwards compatibility is instead covered by the before/after ingestion AC on E001). No soft-AC misfiling: E003 explicitly states COO adoption \"is not a completion condition,\" and E001's dev-cluster validation and SRE hand-off are conditions on the epic's own deliverables. No draft-ID (E00x) references appear in any epic body.\n\n### 7. Completeness (0/2)\nAll strategy HLRs and acceptance criteria are otherwise accounted for (embedded-identity conversion, DSCI migration, service/module/cloudmanager `resourceKind`, action files, cluster-scoped handling, documentation). However, the strategy's Backwards Compatibility NFR — with its verification method \"before/after log output comparison ... confirm ... log consumer (Loki, CloudWatch) ingests both formats without configuration changes\" and its risk mitigation \"deploy a test build to a dev cluster with the production log consumer stack configured ... coordinate with SRE/Support to identify and update any text-based alert rules\" — applies to both binaries (CloudWatch is the cloud-side consumer for cloudmanager on EKS/AKS/CoreWeave, and E002 removes embedded identity from cloudmanager message strings, which is exactly the regression risk described). The decomposition summary's DAG Justification explicitly asserts this is \"an acceptance criterion on each conversion epic, with fix-and-retry handling\" — but E002 has neither the log-consumer regression validation nor the SRE/Support alert-rule identification in its Scope or Acceptance Criteria; only E001 carries them. This is simultaneously a dropped NFR verification requirement on a P0 epic and a factual contradiction between the summary's claim and the actual epic content. Major issue — criterion scores 0. Correction: add the log-consumer regression validation scope item and AC (before/after ingestion comparison for the cloudmanager binary's consumer stack, e.g. CloudWatch) plus the SRE/Support alert-rule identification hand-off to E002 — or, if the omission is deliberate (e.g. no non-OpenShift dev environment), record that decision and its rationale explicitly in E002 and cor