{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a87a54d94d67a9d3","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8010\n3\tscore: 12\n4\tpass: false\n5\trecommendation: revise\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: major\n8\t  criterion: Completeness\n9\t  description: 'E002 (cloudmanager) is missing the log-consumer regression validation\n10\t    and the SRE/Support alert-rule identification that the strategy Backwards Compatibility\n11\t    NFR mandates (it names CloudWatch, cloudmanager consumer) and that the decomposition\n12\t    summary explicitly claims is an acceptance criterion on each conversion epic —\n13\t    only E001 carries them. Correction: add the before/after ingestion-comparison\n14\t    AC and alert-rule identification hand-off to E002, or record a deliberate exclusion\n15\t    rationale and fix the summary claim.'\n16\terror: null\n17\t---\n18\t## Review Summary\n19\t\n20\tScore: 12/14 — fail\n21\tRecommendation: revise\n22\t\n23\tContract validator: OK (all mechanically decidable checks passed: epic body sections, canonical component names, Rule 11 docs-last coverage, priority inheritance, implementation-type parallelism, shared-surface enforcement deliverables).\n24\t\n25\tOne major issue found in Completeness (criterion 7), which forces that criterion to 0 and the overall review to fail per the auto-fail rule. All other criteria score 2.\n26\t\n27\t## Criterion Details\n28\t\n29\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n30\tAll 8 HLRs are mapped in the traceability matrix and in each epic's HLR Traceability section: HLR-1 through HLR-4 and HLR-6 (P0) to E001, HLR-1/HLR-5 (P0) to E002, HLR-8 (P1) to E003. HLR-7 (P1, `resourceKind` on DSC/component controllers) is bundled into E001 with an inline `(bundled)` flag and self-contained rationale in the matrix row — this is the compliant bundling-recording shape, and the rationale is sound: the HLR-7 work is one more key-value pair on the same `log.Error` lines already being edited for HLR-1/HLR-4, i.e., incidental polish on the P0 work rather than a distinct deferrable feature. Not priority collapse. No P2 HLRs exist. Priority inheritance: E001 and E002 are P0; E003 is a docs-authoring epic whose P1 correctly derives from the strategy's Major priority (docs-authoring epics are exempt from priority inheritance).\n31\t\n32\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n33\tDAG is E001 → E003 and E002 → E003, matching E003's frontmatter `dependencies: [RHAISTRAT-8010-E001, RHAISTRAT-8010-E002]` and E001/E002's empty dependency lists exactly; diagram arrow direction (dependency → dependent) is correct. Both Rule 11 edges (all Implementation epics → docs-authoring) are present and justified. No circular dependencies, no invalid edges, no branch files. E001 ∥ E002 parallelism is correctly justified by Rule 15 (same repo, disjoint binaries/controller sets). Critical path length 2 is appropriate for an S-sized strategy (heuristic 1-2). Cross-check for uncaptured data/artifact dependencies: E003's before/after examples and DSCI migration guide consume the conversions' output, and the edges to both conversion epics exist. No konflux/onboarding epics exist, so Rules 7/8/10 parallelism is not applicable.\n34\t\n35\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n36\tE001 and E002 are split by binary surface (manager vs. cloudmanager) with disjoint file sets — a valid same-repo split, not a boundary violation; no epic spans multiple teams (all ODH Operator team) or components inappropriately (E003's Documentation component is the correct canonical mapping for docs-authoring work). The shared-surface CI lint rule is correctly divided per surface into each conversion epic's Scope/ACs rather than carved into a standalone enforcement epic. No scope duplication between E001 and E002 (each covers its own binary's call sites, lint coverage, and unit tests). Work-product types are not mixed — content authoring is separated into E003. E001's size overage (~1.25-1.5 sprints vs. the ~2-week guideline) is shape (b): a recorded estimate, a concrete why-not-split rationale (one mechanical pass over the same call sites; tier split would re-audit the same files), and a concrete conditional re-split trigger in Health Warnings. Recorded as a watch item, not docked.\n37\t\n38\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n39\tNo Investigation epics — and the summary justifies this by evaluating each of the four open questions against the \"does the answer change which downstream epics exist or what they do?\" test, with per-question reasoning (volume vs. structure). All three epics are Implementation and produce concrete artifacts (converted code + lint rule + tests; published guideline). No `gated_by`/`gate_failure_impact` fields are set anywhere, so no gating-metadata consistency issues arise. E003's `implementation_type: docs-authoring` is correct.\n40\t\n41\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n42\tAll three epics are Implementation type and carry `ai_signals` (the 9-signal rubric) — the type-appropriate field; `investigation_signals` is correctly null. Signal values are consistent with the rationales in the ai-signals files. E001's `open_questions: 1` is defensible: the rationale addresses each remaining strategy open question and correctly classifies them as volume/validation concerns rather than approach changes. E003's `open_questions: 0` and `external_dependency: 0` both carry justified rationales (the PM extension-fields question; COO tracking-issue filing). Calibration check on `architecture_claims`: the strategy makes architecture claims (controller tiers, CRDs, cloudmanager binary) and an architecture-context file covers rhods-operator; both E001 and E002 score `architecture_claims: 1` with rationales citing the actual provisioned file (`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/rhods-operator.md`, which exists and covers the cloudmanager binary and controller-runtime/logr usage) — properly calibrated, not a \"no claims\" miss. Computed `ai_implementability`/`ai_implementability_score` values were not checked per review scope.\n43\t\n44\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n45\tEvery epic has testable ACs with explicit measurement methods (lint rule/static analysis, unit tests parsing test logger output, codebase grep). Rule-mandated ACs: E003 (docs-authoring) has the technical-review-before-publication AC; no konflux chain exists; the conversion work is an additive log-call change, not a component replacement, so the rollback/feature-flag rule does not apply (backwards compatibility is instead covered by the before/after ingestion AC on E001). No soft-AC misfiling: E003 explicitly states COO adoption \"is not a completion condition,\" and E001's dev-cluster validation and SRE hand-off are conditions on the epic's own deliverables. No draft-ID (E00x) references appear in any epic body.\n46\t\n47\t### 7. Completeness (0/2)\n48\tAll strategy HLRs and acceptance criteria are otherwise accounted for (embedded-identity conversion, DSCI migration, service/module/cloudmanager `resourceKind`, action files, cluster-scoped handling, documentation). However, the strategy's Backwards Compatibility NFR — with its verification method \"before/after log output comparison ... confirm ... log consumer (Loki, CloudWatch) ingests both formats without configuration changes\" and its risk mitigation \"deploy a test build to a dev cluster with the production log consumer stack configured ... coordinate with SRE/Support to identify and update any text-based alert rules\" — applies to both binaries (CloudWatch is the cloud-side consumer for cloudmanager on EKS/AKS/CoreWeave, and E002 removes embedded identity from cloudmanager message strings, which is exactly the regression risk described). The decomposition summary's DAG Justification explicitly asserts this is \"an acceptance criterion on each conversion epic, with fix-and-retry handling\" — but E002 has neither the log-consumer regression validation nor the SRE/Support alert-rule identification in its Scope or Acceptance Criteria; only E001 carries them. This is simultaneously a dropped NFR verification requirement on a P0 epic and a factual contradiction between the summary's claim and the actual epic content. Major issue — criterion scores 0. Correction: add the log-consumer regression validation scope item and AC (before/after ingestion comparison for the cloudmanager binary's consumer stack, e.g. CloudWatch) plus the SRE/Support alert-rule identification hand-off to E002 — or, if the omission is deliberate (e.g. no non-OpenShift dev environment), record that decision and its rationale explicitly in E002 and correct the summary's \"each conversion epic\" claim.\n49\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b30827ec944669d6","agent_id":"a06e84b5e136515f8","session_id":"24eb6c29-6a9a-48c1-874c-32ca163b119c","uuid":"973865b8-acc8-4810-8faf-7a636f8306eb","timestamp":"2026-10-26T19:46:30.291Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a87a54d94d67a9d3","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8010 decomposition"}
