{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9af445e90b7ae060","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8010-E001\n3\ttitle: Convert manager binary reconciliation error logs to structured resource context\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8010\n5\tcomponent: OpenShift AI Productization\n6\tteam: ODH Operator team\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies: []\n10\tai_signals:\n11\t  change_specificity: 1\n12\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n13\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n14\t  existing_foundation: 1\n15\t  open_questions: 1\n16\t  external_dependency: 1\n17\t  human_process_gates: 0\n18\t  repo_access: 1\n19\t  architecture_claims: 1\n20\timplementation_type: null\n21\tinvestigation_signals: null\n22\tai_implementability: High\n23\tai_implementability_score: 7\n24\tjira_key: null\n25\tbranch: null\n26\tgated_by: null\n27\tgate_failure_impact: null\n28\t---\n29\t# Convert manager binary reconciliation error logs to structured resource context\n30\t\n31\t## Description\n32\t\n33\tThis epic delivers the structured-logging conversion for the rhods-operator manager binary. All reconciliation error logs across the manager's five controller tiers — the DataScienceCluster (DSC) controller, 8 component controllers (DataSciencePipelines, Kueue, ModelRegistry, Ray, SparkOperator, Trainer, TrainingOperator, TrustyAI), the module controller with its 8 module handlers (Dashboard, AIGateway, KServe, OGX, Workbenches, MLflowOperator, FeastOperator, MCPLifecycleOperator), the DSCInitialization (DSCI) controller, and 5 service controllers (Gateway, Auth, Monitoring, CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup) — plus all 15 action files across the four manager-side tiers (8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3 module) emit resource identity as structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`) instead of embedding it in message strings. The DSCI controller's non-standard key names (`Request.Name`, `DSCInitialization`) are migrated to the standard camelCase convention.\n34\t\n35\t## Scope\n36\t\n37\t- Audit all `log.Error()` call sites in reconciliation error paths across the manager binary's controllers and action files, using grep/static analysis to produce a tracked checklist of call sites (confirming the strategy's ~75% embedded-identity estimate).\n38\t- For each call site embedding resource name or namespace in the message string, extract the identity fields and convert to structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`), removing the redundant identity from the message text.\n39\t- Migrate the DSCI controller's structured field names from `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` to `name`/`namespace`.\n40\t- Add `resourceKind` as a structured field to all reconciliation error logs: service controllers, module controller (all 8 module handlers), DSCI, and action files where kind is not implied by controller name (P0), and DSC + component controllers for cross-controller queryability (P1, same call sites).\n41\t- Action files in scope: 8 component action files (`components/<name>/<name>_controller_actions.go`), 3 service action files (`services/{auth,gateway,monitoring}/*_controller_actions.go`), 1 DSC action file (`datasciencecluster/datasciencecluster_controller_actions.go`), 3 module action files (`modules/modules_controller_actions*.go`).\n42\t- Handle cluster-scoped resources (e.g., DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization): emit `name` and `resourceKind`, with `namespace` omitted or set to empty string — no spurious namespace value.\n43\t- Author and verify the CI lint rule (or static-analysis check) covering the manager binary's converted call sites: it flags any `log.Error` call in the manager binary with name/namespace embedded in message text or missing required structured fields.\n44\t- Unit tests asserting field presence and correctness via a test logger for each controller tier and action-file tier.\n45\t- Log consumer regression validation: deploy a test build to a dev cluster with the production log consumer stack configured, trigger sample reconciliation errors, and compare before/after log output — structured fields present, and the log consumer (Loki, CloudWatch) ingests both formats without configuration changes.\n46\t- Coordinate with SRE/Support to identify text-based alert rules that rely on the changed message-string patterns (identification and hand-off only; updating the rules is SRE-owned and out of scope).\n47\t\n48\t## Acceptance Criteria\n49\t\n50\t- Given any manager-binary reconciliation error log call that previously embedded resource name or namespace in the message string, when the code is updated, then the call uses structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`), measured by the CI lint rule or static analysis flagging zero remaining `log.Error` calls in the manager binary with name/namespace embedded in message text.\n51\t- Given the DSCI controller's reconciliation error logs, when the code is updated, then all structured field names use the camelCase convention (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`), measured by unit test asserting DSCI error logs contain the standard field names and a codebase grep confirming no references to `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` as log keys remain.\n52\t- Given a service controller (Gateway, Auth, Monitoring, CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup) encounters a reconciliation error, when the error is logged, then the entry includes `resourceKind`, measured by unit test parsing test logger output and asserting `resourceKind` is present with the correct value.\n53\t- Given an action-file error log across any of the 4 tiers (8 component, 3 service, 1 DSC, 3 module), when the code is updated, then the entry includes `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind`, measured by unit test asserting all three fields are present with correct values across all 15 action files.\n54\t- Given the module controller reconciles any of its 8 module handlers and encounters an error, when the error is logged, then the entry includes `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind`, measured by unit test asserting field presence and correctness.\n55\t- Given DSC and component controller reconciliation error logs, when the code is updated, then entries include `resourceKind` for cross-controller queryability, measured by unit test asserting `resourceKind` presence.\n56\t- Given a cluster-scoped resource (e.g., DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization) is reconciled and encounters an error, when the error is logged, then the entry includes `name` and `resourceKind` with `namespace` omitted or empty, measured by unit test confirming no spurious namespace value.\n57\t- The CI lint rule covering the manager binary's call sites runs in CI and passes on the converted codebase, and fails on a deliberately re-introduced message-embedded identity call.\n58\t- Before/after log output comparison for a sample reconciliation error from a dev-cluster test build confirms structured fields appear and the production log consumer stack ingests both formats without configuration changes.\n59\t- A list of identified text-based alert rules relying on changed message-string patterns is delivered to SRE/Support.\n60\t\n61\t## HLR Traceability\n62\t\n63\t- HLR-1 (P0) — conversion of message-embedded name/namespace to structured KV pairs in manager-binary reconciliation error logs (manager portion).\n64\t- HLR-2 (P0) — DSCI controller migrated from `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` to camelCase convention.\n65\t- HLR-3 (P0) — service controller error logs include `resourceKind`.\n66\t- HLR-4 (P0) — all action-file error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` across all 4 tiers (8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3 module).\n67\t- HLR-6 (P0) — module controller error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` (8 module handlers).\n68\t- HLR-7 (P1) — DSC and component controller error logs include `resourceKind` (bundled: same `log.Error` call sites as the HLR-1/HLR-4 conversions; adding one key-value pair to lines already being edited — a separate epic would re-audit and re-edit the same files twice).\n69\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-83fff1f27ccb2d1e","agent_id":"ab4fe27734f626708","session_id":"24eb6c29-6a9a-48c1-874c-32ca163b119c","uuid":"20e371f4-b44b-4366-95d3-2a4ad6885200","timestamp":"2026-10-26T19:47:44.547Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-9af445e90b7ae060","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8010 decomposition"}
