{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a59f08f0aee0820d","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8010-E002\n3\ttitle: Convert cloudmanager binary reconciliation error logs to structured resource\n4\t  context\n5\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8010\n6\tcomponent: OpenShift AI Productization\n7\tteam: ODH Operator team\n8\ttype: Implementation\n9\tpriority: P0\n10\tdependencies: []\n11\tai_signals:\n12\t  change_specificity: 1\n13\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n14\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n15\t  existing_foundation: 1\n16\t  open_questions: 1\n17\t  external_dependency: 1\n18\t  human_process_gates: 0\n19\t  repo_access: 1\n20\t  architecture_claims: 1\n21\timplementation_type: null\n22\tinvestigation_signals: null\n23\tai_implementability: High\n24\tai_implementability_score: 7\n25\tjira_key: null\n26\tbranch: null\n27\tgated_by: null\n28\tgate_failure_impact: null\n29\t---\n30\t# Convert cloudmanager binary reconciliation error logs to structured resource context\n31\t\n32\t## Description\n33\t\n34\tThis epic delivers the structured-logging conversion for the rhods-operator's cloudmanager binary, which handles multi-cloud infrastructure dependencies on non-OpenShift Kubernetes (AWS EKS, Azure AKS, CoreWeave). All reconciliation error logs from the cloudmanager's three controllers (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) — built on the same controller-runtime/logr foundation using the shared reconciler pattern — emit resource identity as structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`) instead of embedding it in message strings. These controllers already receive `name`/`namespace` from controller-runtime's request logger via the shared reconciler; the missing pieces are converting any message-embedded identity in error paths and adding `resourceKind` (kind is not implied by controller name for these controllers).\n35\t\n36\t## Scope\n37\t\n38\t- Audit all `log.Error()` call sites in reconciliation error paths across the cloudmanager binary's three controllers, using grep/static analysis to produce a tracked checklist of call sites.\n39\t- For each call site embedding resource name or namespace in the message string, extract the identity fields and convert to structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`), removing the redundant identity from the message text.\n40\t- Add `resourceKind` as a structured field to all cloudmanager reconciliation error logs.\n41\t- Migrate any non-standard structured key names to the camelCase convention (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`), if present in these controllers.\n42\t- Handle cluster-scoped resources appropriately: emit `name` and `resourceKind`, with `namespace` omitted or set to empty string — no spurious namespace value.\n43\t- Author and verify the CI lint rule (or static-analysis check) covering the cloudmanager binary's converted call sites: it flags any `log.Error` call in the cloudmanager binary with name/namespace embedded in message text or missing required structured fields.\n44\t- Unit tests asserting field presence and correctness via a test logger for each of the three controllers.\n45\t- Log consumer regression validation for the cloudmanager binary: deploy a test build to a non-OpenShift dev environment (e.g., an EKS dev cluster) with the production cloud log consumer stack configured (CloudWatch), trigger sample reconciliation errors, and compare before/after log output — structured fields appear, and the log consumer ingests both formats without configuration changes.\n46\t- Coordinate with SRE/Support to identify text-based alert rules that rely on the changed cloudmanager message-string patterns (identification and hand-off only; updating the rules is SRE-owned and out of scope).\n47\t\n48\t## Acceptance Criteria\n49\t\n50\t- Given a cloudmanager controller (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) encounters a reconciliation error, when the error is logged, then the entry includes `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` as structured fields, measured by unit test asserting field presence and correctness in test logger output.\n51\t- Given any cloudmanager reconciliation error log call that previously embedded resource name or namespace in the message string, when the code is updated, then the call uses structured key-value pairs, measured by the CI lint rule or static analysis flagging zero remaining `log.Error` calls in the cloudmanager binary with name/namespace embedded in message text.\n52\t- Given a cluster-scoped infrastructure resource is reconciled and encounters an error, when the error is logged, then the entry includes `name` and `resourceKind` with `namespace` omitted or empty, measured by unit test confirming no spurious namespace value.\n53\t- The CI lint rule covering the cloudmanager binary's call sites runs in CI and passes on the converted codebase, and fails on a deliberately re-introduced message-embedded identity call.\n54\t- Before/after log output comparison for a sample cloudmanager reconciliation error from a test build in a non-OpenShift dev environment confirms structured fields appear and the production cloud log consumer stack (CloudWatch) ingests both formats without configuration changes.\n55\t- A list of identified text-based alert rules relying on changed cloudmanager message-string patterns is delivered to SRE/Support.\n56\t\n57\t## HLR Traceability\n58\t\n59\t- HLR-1 (P0) — conversion of message-embedded name/namespace to structured KV pairs in rhods-operator reconciliation error logs (cloudmanager portion).\n60\t- HLR-5 (P0) — cloudmanager controller error logs include `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind`.\n61\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-83fff1f27ccb2d1e","agent_id":"ab4fe27734f626708","session_id":"24eb6c29-6a9a-48c1-874c-32ca163b119c","uuid":"93658365-06a7-4329-bab4-0894d4ae4bbb","timestamp":"2026-10-26T19:47:45.164Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a59f08f0aee0820d","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8010 decomposition"}
